From 29ba36ead2ce4bd4bfb21662b6f5680f16f4b966 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 22:49:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] rc: Dreadwatch on one table and free lists; Security Review
 protections; store writes via rstore

rwarden.c: Dreadwatch's free lists (URLhaus mirror, phishing, C2 with fronting
lines skipped; crypto, ransomware, CERT Polska, Threatview optional; Reaper's
Basics = the three defaults + the ThreatFox snapshot), filled in priority order
up to 1,000,000 names with a free-memory guard, every list checked by dnsmasq
before install, one awk pass a night, compiled into /tmp/reaper_nx/threat.db
(rebuilt from /jffs/rwarden/dns.k.gz at boot, in the background); the ASUS DDNS
and Let's Encrypt hosts whitelisted once while the certificate is in use; the
never-block names are one-time entries the operator can remove. rwatch.c: the
Security Review watches Reaper's own protection switches, adopts a setting an
older baseline predates and retires one this firmware no longer watches (both
one log line, never a change), reads its settings in one _nvs call, restarts
rednsd, refetches Wallguard's owed lists; history rings trimmed per tenth.
reaper_nv.h: _nvs, one guarded `nvram show` for many keys. reaper_usbmon.c:
publishes the store location and restores Wallguard from the volume.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
